    "subject": {
      "description": "A large sculpture of a crouching cat built from black wire mesh strung with warm fairy lights, glowing red eyes and bared metal fangs, standing on a raised straw-covered bed at a paved street corner at dusk. A man in a dark jacket and knit cap stands beside it for scale, with an interpretive signboard and passersby in winter coats nearby, city buildings and a mural visible behind.",
      "media_analyzed": "photo"
    },
    "subject_description": {
      "standard": "The Jólakötturinn (Yule Cat) sculpture on Lækjargata in downtown Reykjavík — a menacing wire-and-lightbulb Christmas cat from Icelandic folklore, red eyes glowing and fangs bared at dusk, with Hans posing beside it for scale.",
      "monty_python": "INT./EXT. A REYKJAVÍK STREET CORNER, DUSK. A twelve-foot wire cat, wreathed in fairy lights and radiating the quiet menace of a tax auditor, looms over the cobblestones. A MAN in a beanie stands beside it, seemingly unaware that Icelandic folklore holds this creature personally responsible for devouring anyone who fails to receive new clothes by Christmas. 'Lovely evening for a light mauling,' he does not say, having evidently skipped the pamphlet. The cat's eyes glow an ominous red. Somewhere, a sensible Icelandic grandmother knits furiously, muttering about deadlines."
    }
  },
